    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Hangzhou began as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin</a> county of Qiantang, became a prefectural seat under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sui-dynasty">Sui</a> in 589 and was joined to the north by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-canal">Grand Canal</a>, which reached its full length by 609.</li><li>From 1132 until the Mongol conquest of 1276 it was the capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Southern Song</a> as Lin'an, and its population grew far beyond its walls, with estimates of more than one million.</li><li>Under the Yuan and later dynasties it remained a commercial and administrative centre, its harbour silted up by the middle of the Ming, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiping-rebellion">Taiping</a>, warlords and Japanese occupation each brought damage or contest in the 19th and 20th centuries.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Hangzhou's reputation among Europeans was such that it was the intended destination of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christopher-columbus">Christopher Columbus</a>'s first voyage to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a> in 1492 and 1493.</p></aside>
    </section>

