    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">c. 221 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">The First Emperor of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin</a> establishes Qiantang as a county</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">589</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sui-dynasty">Sui</a> make Hangzhou the seat of the prefecture of Hang</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1089</span><span class="rf-text">Su Shi builds a causeway across West Lake with 200,000 workers</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1132</span><span class="rf-text">Hangzhou is chosen as the capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Southern Song</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1208</span><span class="rf-text">The worst of the city's great fires burns for four days</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1276</span><span class="rf-text">Mongol armies under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kublai-khan">Kublai Khan</a> besiege and capture the city</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1860</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiping-rebellion">Taiping</a> Heavenly Kingdom occupies Hangzhou for the second time</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1949</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peoples-liberation-army">People's Liberation Army</a> enters Hangzhou on 3 May</span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="origins">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Origins</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/grand-canal"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/grand-canal.jpg" alt="Grand Canal" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/grand-canal">Grand Canal</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Wanning_Bridge_1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">EditQ, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Hangzhou stands at the southern end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-canal">Grand Canal</a> and at the head of Hangzhou Bay, in northwestern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhejiang">Zhejiang</a> province, with its centre built around the eastern and northern sides of West Lake, just north of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qiantang-river">Qiantang River</a>. People lived in the region long before any city. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neolithic">Neolithic</a> Hemudu culture inhabited Yuyao, 100 km south-east of Hangzhou, as far back as seven thousand years ago, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> was first cultivated in southeast <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> during that time. Excavations have established that the jade-carving Liangzhu culture, named for its type site just northwest of the present city, inhabited the area immediately around it about five thousand years ago.</p><p>The first of Hangzhou's present neighbourhoods to appear in written records was Yuhang, which probably preserves an old <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baiyue">Baiyue</a> name. The First Emperor of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin</a> established Qiantang as a county under the direction of Kuaiji <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/commandery">Commandery</a>, now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shaoxing">Shaoxing</a>, in the area of the Wulin Mountains and the Wulin Lakes. Under the Han the same area was known as Wulin. In 589 CE the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sui-dynasty">Sui</a> made it the seat of the prefecture of Hang, which entitled it to a city wall, built two years later. By a longstanding convention also seen in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guangzhou">Guangzhou</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fuzhou">Fuzhou</a>, the city took the name of the area it administered and became known as Hangzhou.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="growth">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Growth &amp; Great Ages</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Hangzhou lay at the southern end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-canal">Grand Canal</a>, which extends to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a> and evolved over centuries, reaching its full length by 609. In 610 the Jiangnan Canal was dredged and widened along an existing channel to connect <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suzhou">Suzhou</a> with Hangzhou. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tang-dynasty">Tang dynasty</a> the poet Bai Juyi, as governor, saw that the farmland nearby depended on the water of West Lake and that its old dyke had collapsed through negligence, leaving the farmers in severe drought. He ordered a stronger and taller dyke with a dam to control the flow, and the livelihood of local people improved over the following years.</p><p>In the tenth century Hangzhou, then named Xifu, was one of the three great bastions of culture in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nanjing">Nanjing</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chengdu">Chengdu</a>. The leaders of Wuyue were noted patrons of the arts, particularly of Buddhist temple architecture and artwork, and the city drew scholars from throughout China and conducted diplomacy with neighbouring Chinese states, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>, Goryeo and the Khitan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liao-dynasty">Liao dynasty</a>. In 1089 the poet and governor Su Shi used 200,000 workers to build a causeway 2.8 km long across West Lake, made of mud dredged from the lake bottom.</p><p>Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Southern Song</a>, commercial expansion, an influx of refugees from the north and the growth of the official and military establishments increased the population, and the city developed well outside its 9th-century ramparts. The city remained an important port until the middle of the Ming era, when its harbour slowly silted up.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="rulers">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Who Ruled It</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Qiantang was a county of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin</a>, and the area was called Wulin under the Han. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sui-dynasty">Sui</a> made it a prefectural seat in 589. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/five-dynasties-and-ten-kingdoms-period">Five Dynasties and Ten Kingdoms period</a> Hangzhou, then called Xifu, was the capital of the kingdom of Wuyue. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Southern Song</a> chose it as their capital in 1132, after most of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> had been conquered by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jurchens">Jurchens</a> in the Jin-Song wars and the imperial family had retreated south from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaifeng">Kaifeng</a>, captured in the Jingkang Incident of 1127. Emperor Gaozong moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nanjing">Nanjing</a>, then to modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shangqiu">Shangqiu</a>, then to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yangzhou">Yangzhou</a> in 1128, and finally to Hangzhou in 1129.</p><p>From 1132 until the Mongol invasion of 1276 Hangzhou remained the capital of the Southern Song and was known as Lin'an. It was the seat of the imperial government, a centre of trade and entertainment and the nexus of the main branches of the civil service. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> took it in 1276. The capital of the new <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuan-dynasty">Yuan dynasty</a> was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dadu">Dadu</a>, now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a>, but Hangzhou remained an important commercial and administrative centre for the Yuan's southern territory. Under the Qing it was the site of an imperial army garrison.</p><p>After the collapse of the Qing, warlords, particularly those of the Anhui and Zhili cliques, contested the city until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuomintang">Kuomintang</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/northern-expedition">Northern Expedition</a>. The Republic of China then administered it more fully from 1927 to 1937. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> occupied it from 1937 to 1945, the Kuomintang returned and governed until 3 May 1949, and then the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peoples-liberation-army">People's Liberation Army</a> entered and the city came under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chinese-communist-party">Chinese Communist Party</a> control.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Hangzhou&#8221; cites where it covers Song dynasty, Yuan dynasty:</p><ul><li>Frederick W. Mote, Imperial China: 900–1800 (Harvard University Press, 2003)</li><li>Arthur Christopher Moule, Quinsai (Cambridge University Press, 1957)</li><li>George Lane, The Phoenix Mosque and the Persians of Medieval Hangzhou (University of Chicago Press, 2018)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="life">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Life in the City</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/odoric-of-pordenone"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/odoric-of-pordenone.jpg" alt="Odoric of Pordenone" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/odoric-of-pordenone">Odoric of Pordenone</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:DepartureOfOdoricOfPordenone.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>When the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Southern Song</a> made Hangzhou a permanent capital, its imperial palace, modest in size, was expanded in 1133 with new roofed alleyways and in 1148 with an extension of the walls. The city walls, of tamped earth and stone, were 30 feet high and 10 feet thick at the base, with 13 gates and several towers, and covered four miles north to south and only one mile east to west. Philosophers, politicians and men of letters, among them the poets Su Shi, Lu You and Xin Qiji, came to live and die there, and the scientist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shen-kuo">Shen Kuo</a> was born there and buried in the Yuhang district. Arab merchants lived in the city during the Song, when ocean trade took precedence over land trade, and a Persian settler built the Phoenix Mosque.</p><p>Travellers of the Yuan period described the city as one of the foremost in the world. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marco-polo">Marco Polo</a>, who supposedly visited in the late 13th century, called it greater than any in the world, although his account greatly exaggerates its size. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odoric-of-pordenone">Odoric of Pordenone</a> also called it the greatest city in the world, and noted that bread, pork, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> and wine were abundant. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-battuta">Ibn Battuta</a> visited in 1345 and noted the many well-made, painted wooden ships on the canals,.</p><p>In 1848 Hangzhou was described as the stronghold of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhejiang">Zhejiang</a>, with several mosques bearing Arabic inscriptions. It was an important centre of Chinese Jewry as late as the 16th and 17th centuries, and its Immaculate Conception Cathedral dates back 400 years to the Ming. Lingyin Temple was founded in 326 CE. Residents speak the Hangzhou dialect, a Wu dialect.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="crises">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Sieges &amp; Disasters</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/kublai-khan"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kublai-khan.jpg" alt="Kublai Khan" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/kublai-khan">Kublai Khan</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:YuanEmperorAlbumKhubilaiPortrait.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Araniko, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Hangzhou was particularly vulnerable to fire, because of its large population and densely crowded, often multi-storey wooden buildings. Major fires destroyed large sections of the city in 1208, 1229, 1237 and 1275. The fire of 1237 alone destroyed 30,000 dwellings. The worst was that of 1208, which burned for four days over a diameter of three miles and burned 58,097 houses, killing 59 people. To combat the threat, the city built storage buildings that were rented out to merchants and patrolled by watchmen at night, and it was enclosed by water on all sides. The government also set up an elaborate fire-fighting system, with watchtowers, lantern and flag signals to identify the source of the flames and direct the response, and more than 3,000 soldiers charged with putting out fires.</p><p>In 1276 the advancing Mongol armies of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kublai-khan">Kublai Khan</a> besieged and captured Hangzhou, three years before the final collapse of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Southern Song</a>. Historian Patricia Buckley Ebrey noted that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> killed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jurchens">Jurchen</a> Wanyan royal family by the hundreds in the siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaifeng">Kaifeng</a> in 1232 but spared Hangzhou, including the Zhao royal family of the Southern Song. The Mongols rehired Southern Song officials.</p><p>In 1856 and 1860 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiping-rebellion">Taiping</a> Heavenly Kingdom occupied Hangzhou, and the city was heavily damaged during its conquest, occupation and eventual reconquest by the Qing army. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> occupied it from 1937 to 1945. During the late <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cultural-revolution">Cultural Revolution</a> Hangzhou was the stage of labour unrest and factional fighting known as the 1975 Hangzhou incident.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/baochu-pagoda"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/baochu-pagoda.jpg" alt="Baochu Pagoda" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/baochu-pagoda">Baochu Pagoda</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:BaoChuTa.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Chrisguilbeau, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Hangzhou is counted among the ancient capitals of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, and its poets and governors left marks on its landscape. Bai Juyi had willows and other trees planted along his dyke at West Lake, which became a landmark. The West Lake Cultural Landscape, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-canal">Grand Canal</a>, designated in 2014, and the Archaeological Ruins of Liangzhu City are three <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Sites</a> in the city. Around the lake stand pagodas and temples, among them Lingyin Temple, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baochu-pagoda">Baochu Pagoda</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leifeng-pagoda">Leifeng Pagoda</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yue-fei">Yue Fei</a> Temple, built in 1221 to commemorate the general Yue Fei, who died through political persecution.</p><p>An old Chinese proverb says that there is Heaven above, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suzhou">Suzhou</a> and Hangzhou below. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marco-polo">Marco Polo</a> described Hangzhou as the city of heaven. The accounts of Marco Polo, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odoric-of-pordenone">Odoric of Pordenone</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-battuta">Ibn Battuta</a> carried the city's name to western readers under forms such as Khinzai, Campsay and al-Khansa. The China Academy of Art and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhejiang">Zhejiang</a> University are among the institutions in the city.</p><p>Today Hangzhou is the capital and most populous city of Zhejiang province, a sub-provincial city in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yangtze-river">Yangtze River</a> Delta with more than twelve million permanent residents. It hosted the eleventh G20 summit in 2016. The city still keeps its historical and cultural heritage and its natural setting beside West Lake, despite recent urban development.</p></div><div class="profile-inv"><p class="profile-inv-k">Songs in history</p><p class="profile-inv-l"><a href="/songs/mo-li-hua">Mo Li Hua<span>1765</span></a><a href="/songs/ode-to-the-motherland">Ode to the Motherland<span>1950</span></a></p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Hangzhou, at the southern end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-canal">Grand Canal</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhejiang">Zhejiang</a>, began as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin</a> county of Qiantang. From 1132 to 1276 it was the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Southern Song</a> capital Lin'an, and travellers described it as one of the foremost cities in the world. It is now the capital of Zhejiang province.</em></p>

